{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 32, "model_name": "nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b", "content": "**Cherry Blossoms and City Lights: A Whirlwind Week in Japan**\n\nLanding in Tokyo felt like stepping into a living postcard. The neon glow of Shibuya crossed with the quiet reverence of Meiji Shrine set the tone for a journey that balanced frenetic energy with serene moments. I started my mornings with steaming bowls of ramen at tiny alley‑way stalls, slurping noodles while locals hurried past, their umbrellas a sea of pastel against the drizzle.\n\nA day trip to Hakone offered a stark contrast: the soothing hiss of an onsen, the majestic view of Mt. Fuji framed by lantern‑lit ryokan roofs, and a peaceful cruise on Lake Ashi where the water mirrored the surrounding hills. Even the famous Hakone Open‑Air Museum felt like a walk through a sculpture garden where art and nature conversed effortlessly.\n\nKyoto greeted me with its timeless charm. I wandered the moss‑covered paths of Kinkaku‑ji, watched geishas glide through Gion’s lantern‑lit streets, and participated in a tea ceremony that turned every sip into a meditation. The Fushimi Inari trek—thousands of vermilion torii gates winding up the mountain—was both a workout and a spiritual pilgrimage.\n\nOsaka’s street food scene was the perfect finale. Takoyaki balls sizzling on griddles, okonomiyaki pancakes flipped with flair, and the bustling Dotonbori canal lit up like a carnival at night. Each bite told a story of tradition meeting innovation.\n\nLeaving Japan, I carried more than souvenirs—I carried the rhythm of taiko drums in my heart, the scent of sakura in my memory, and a promise to return, for there’s always another hidden alley, another temple gate, another bowl of waiting ramen.", "logprobs": [{"token": "We", "logprob": -0.11869972944259644}, {"token": " need", "logprob": -0.0025052609853446484}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.08262719959020615}, {"token": " write", "logprob": -0.62